            Debug.Assert(index <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX ? true : false);
            // add an assertion that the index passed in is equal to new index of x in array 
            //Debug.Assert(index == New_arrListIndexOfX ? true : false);
        }
    }
}

```

We are providing this test as a contract test, so we are not modifying the original list, only trying to verify whether get and set methods behave as expected.

Here we are observing the count of items in the list before and after our modification, and we are observing the location of the new value in the list using index and both LastIndexOf and IndexOf operations, and whether the list contains the value.

We are asserting that the set method modifies the correct value, i.e., the index passed in is within the range of LastIndexOf of the value (new index of the value in array after modification).

We still have one assertion for the IndexOf, which is commented because Pex doesn't handle IndexOf correctly, maybe this is a bug or feature. It would require a more complex approach to handle this.

Remember that the Pex is only used for exploration, it is not intended to cover all possible cases. Therefore, always review the generated tests. Adjust the assumptions and assertions to fit the specific needs of your project.

It is recommended to use NUnit or MSTest for unit testing. Pex has good support for those, and some developers find it more powerful.

And as was mentioned before, use the PexCustomization.cs file to customize the tests to fit into your project or organization's style and standards.

This should be more than enough to get you started with automatic testing using Pex, and hopefully with this you'll be able to introduce automated testing into your projects.

Note: Be aware that Pex generates a large number of tests, and running them on your computer can take a long time and your computer might get slow or hang.

Avoid running tests on production code, and always use a separate dev/test environment to test your code.

Finally, be mindful of the possibility of false positives and false negatives. Both of these can be caused by Pex. It's important to understand the implications and how to handle them efficiently.

For more details on how to use Pex please look at the official Microsoft website or on some of the many other sources.

Pex can be a real time-saver, but also can be quite helpful for catching bugs that you might have overlooked. The important thing is to be thoughtful about using it and understanding the implications.

Hope this helps you. Good luck with testing your code.

