So in this case, we used PexInstrumentation to instrument arraylist object and then observed the changes in the properties of arraylist like IndexOf, LastIndexOf, Count and Contains on certain index and then did the assertions to make the test case more robust and provide better coverage.
Now, when you execute this Pex method, then it will automatically try out various scenarios for your code covering various corner cases and ensuring that the change in elements is as expected based on the index passed into Set method.
One thing to note is that PexInstrumentation should be enabled in the project where you're instrumenting the object and that it cannot replace manual unit testing but aids in the process of testing. It's a powerful tool which can handle complex situations quite well for code coverage but won't completely eliminate the necessity for manual verification of test cases.

A: There are a few issues with your PexInstrumentation.


*

*You're trying to access a generic type parameter which is not defined in the method signature of PexInstrumentedMethod you have defined. The ArrayList requires a type parameter. You should've included it in the arraylist parameter like this: [PexInstrumented("System.Collections.ArrayList`1[[System.Int32]]")] 

*If your method should only be run on inputs that meet a condition, use [PexAssumeUnderTest] or ensure that your method is only be run on those inputs that pass your AssumePrecondition checks.


You also missed a few other parts like the parameters and assertions. Just in case you do not have such details, here's an example of what the PexInstrumentation attribute could look like:

[PexMethodUnderTest]
        [PexInstrumented("System.Collections.ArrayList`1[[System.Int32]]")] 
        public void YourMethodName([PexAssumeUnderTest("index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count")] int index, [PexAssumeUnderTest("x > 0 && x <= 100")] int x, ArrayList arrList)
        {
            // Code 
        }

Note: For this attribute to succeed, Microsoft.Pex.Framework must be installed in your project and turned on for Pex to work.
Please check the docs for more detailed examples and tips for proper usage: https://github.com/Microsoft/Partnership-Writing-Tools/blob/master/Doc/features/PexInstrumentation.md 
Hope these suggestions help to point you in the right direction for your code coverage.

A: If you are using the PexRunner, then you will execute your tests using this class with the Run method on any method that requires testing. 
To use PexInstrumentation you have to decorate your test methods with the [PexMethodUnderTest] attribute and if you want to check the property changes along with the method execution you have to decorate your method parameters with [PexInstrumented("fullTypeName")]. 

If you also want to add assumptions and observe the results of these assumptions too, you can use [PexAssumeUnderTest] and [PexObserve] respectively. 

Here’s the example of how this can be done:

[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[Tag("PexExploratory")]
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexInstrumentAssembly(AssemblyNames = new[] { "YourProjectAssemblyName" })]
public void YourTestMethod([PexAssumeUnderTest("YourCondition")] int index, [PexInstrumented("YourProjectAssemblyName+YourClassName")] ArrayList arrList) {
//Your Code
}

Full Type Name Format: Full assembly name followed by the full type name. E.g., if your class is in namespace Test and assembly name is TestAssembly, your full type name will be TestAssembly+Test+YourClassName.

Remember to add the Microsoft.Pex and Microsoft.Pex.Framework references to your test project.

