	   // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ array list contains x after setting new value 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ array list does not contain oldvalue after setting new value @@@
            bool doesNotContain = false;
            for (int i = 0; i < arrList.Count; ++i)
            {
                if ((int) arrList[i] == Old_x)
                {
                    doesNotContain = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            Debug.Assert(doesNotContain);
        }
    }
}
```
The above code is a contract test for the `PUT_SetContract` method of the `ArrayList` class. 

It assumes that an instance of ArrayList `arrList` with at least one element, and two integers `x` and `index` that satisfy the premise `index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count`.

The test firstly stores the count of `arrList`, `IndexOf(x)`, `LastIndexOf(x)` and `Contains(x)` before and after the execution of `arrList[index] = x;`. If the list contains `x` after `index` is set, the `ArrayList` has not been changed since it results in not adding but removing an element from list and the count will also not changed.

Then it checks the following preconditions:
- `New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1`, indicating that the count of `arrList` decreases by 1.
- `arrList[index] == x`, indicating that the value at `index` is set to `x` correctly.
- `New_arrListIndexOfX > Old_arrListIndexOfX`, indicating that `x` is in the list again (maybe at different `index` if previous exist).
- `New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX`, indicating that `x` is still at the last occurrence location or later in the list.

The test fails if the above conditions are not met.

Note: More detailed logic may need to be implemented according to actual actual behaviors.

## 5. Test Suite Design 

Test Suite in Pex/Drivers is organized by categories, where each category can have sub-categories. The test cases are organized within these categories and sub-categories, making it easier to find test cases that cover the specific functionalities you are testing against.

The default Test Categories are:
- FuncTests – all functional test cases
- StressTests – test cases to ensure your code can handle a range of different input conditions and scenarios
- Performance Tests – test cases intended to measure the performance characteristics of your system under a specific operational condition
- Regression Tests - tests that ensure that the fixed defect in the old version is being fixed
- Compatibility Tests – tests based on the requirement set to ensure that one of the system does not fail in compatibility
- Security Tests – examine your product to test whether it conforms to standard compliance and security guidelines 

In addition, test suite can consist of individual test methods corresponding to each class/method in your code.

## 6. Test Management

Pex/Drivers provides a powerful visual approach of managing test cases, organizing them in project and category, tracking their statuses, assigning responsiblities, etc.

You can use labels to enhance the clarity of the test case organization. For example, there could be a separate “Regression_Tests” label applied to all regression test cases, and a “P1--------------------            // should have been added @@@ (arrList.Contains(x), arrList.IndexOf(x), arrList.LastIndexOf(x), arrList.Count)

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if new arrList.Contains(x) is false then indexOf(x) equals -1 @@@ (arrList.Contains(x), arrList.IndexOf(x))

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ if old count > new count then element with index index should've been removed @@@ (arrList.Count, index, Old_arrListCount, arrList[index])

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ if old count < new count then element with index index should've been added @@@ (arrList.Count, index, Old_arrListCount, arrList[index])

        }
    }
}

{% endhighlight %}

You should add your assert in the placeholders. The tests framework includes predefined helper methods (as seen in the comments) that help you to test the behavior of the mutant.

This Pex test validates that the `ArrayList` behaves correctly after `Set` operation (changing an element at a specific index). The tests capture initial and final array state, checks if count was changed as expected and if elements were correctly changed. If the count changed and the elements were updated, but the index of updated element is greater than (or equals) than the old count, then the tests would have failed as well because element with such index doesn't exist in the array. 

## Add Mutant

Below is a Mutant test for adding a value to the `ArrayList`:

{% highlight csharp %}

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            arrList.Add(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrLIstContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_lastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that number of elements in the array has been increased @@@ (arrList.Count, Old_arrListCount)

	    // assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that array list contains 'x' @@@ (arrList.Contains(x))

	    // assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the last index of 'x' equals index of new added element @@@ (arrList.LastIndexOf(x), New_arrListLastIndexOfX)

        }
    }
}

{% endhighlight %}

Same as for `Set` operation, you need to cover different cases and validate that `ArrayList` behaves correctly after `Add` operation. The test checks that new item was added and the count of the array was increased. 

